This study introduces a novel parent selection mechanism designed to mitigate evaluation time bias in asynchronous parallel multi-objective evolutionary algorithms. The proposed method, termed Frequency-centered Parent Selection (FPC), operates by assigning a frequency value to each individual based on its age and the count of its offspring. This frequency value is then utilized to determine the probability of an individual being chosen as a parent. The paper provides a theoretical analysis of FPC and demonstrates its effectiveness through experiments on the CEC2017 multi-objective benchmark suite. Results indicate that FPC significantly reduces evaluation time bias, thereby enhancing the performance of asynchronous parallel multi-objective evolutionary algorithms. Furthermore, the study examines the impact of varying the frequency value calculation method on FPC's performance. Finally, the paper concludes with a discussion of the findings and outlines potential directions for future research. The source code is accessible at https://github.com/chenyong1992/FPC.

